\title{DFS terminates}
\date{2026-09-18T13:12:06Z}
\tag{algorithms}
\taxon{Theorem}

\import{algorithms-macros}

\p{For any finite graph #{G} and any unvisited vertex #{u}, #{\DFS(G,u)}: \ul{\li{terminates in finite time, and} \li{decreases the number of unvisited vertices.}}}

\proof{
  By strong induction on the number of unvisited vertices.
  \ul{
    \li{If #{u} is the only unvisited vertex, then the \sf{if} statement inside the loop will never be true, so \DFS will never call itself recursively and will terminate after iterating through all the neighbors of #{u} (since #{G} is finite, #{u} can only have a finite number of neighbors).  It also marks #{u} visited, thus decreasing the number of unvisited vertices.}
    \li{Otherwise, the first action of #{\DFS(G,u)} is to mark #{u} visited, thus decreasing the number of unvisited vertices by one.  Each recursive call to an unvisited neighbor #{\DFS(G,v)} will be with fewer unvisited vertices, and therefore by the induction hypothesis will terminate and reduce the number of unvisited vertices further.}
  }
}

\transclude{00ME}  % also terminates when called on visited vertex
